{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/25950-pantheon-sepcific-books","id":25950,"title":"Pantheon sepcific books","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-12-08T12:23:03-08:00","id":25950,"title":"Pantheon sepcific books","content":"Not only do I want I new version of Scion: Ragnarok for the new edition, I want several pantheon specific books covering the major pantheons. Obviously of the ten realworld Pantheons outlined in Scion so far, some wouldn'd be able to carry a book of their own for financial reasons, so the less popular Patheon might be placed in a book together. It wouldn't be as good as a full book of their own, but it would be very useful.<br /> <br /> The main use of each of these books should be to make the myths and stories of that pantheon easier to roleplay. The kinds of problems and styles of stories should be a major theme in each book.<br /> <br /> Also as each pantheon has different types of supporting casts and monsters, different groups will need to be stressed in each book. Examples, both the Greek and the Japanese gods have large groups of allied gods. But the Greek gods have the Teelchines as Frenemies of a unique sort. Both the Loa and The Tuatha have many closely allied pantheons that would need discussion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":857,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","user":"Astromancer","date":". 12-08-2013, 03:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-08T12:46:38-08:00","id":25968,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/25950-pantheon-sepcific-books#post25950\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not only do I want I new version of Scion: Ragnarok for the new edition, I want several pantheon specific books covering the major pantheons. Obviously of the ten realworld Pantheons outlined in Scion so far, <b>some wouldn'd be able to carry a book of their own for financial reasons</b>, so the less popular Patheon might be placed in a book together. It wouldn't be as good as a full book of their own, but it would be very useful.</div> </div> </div> </div>Serious question: what leads you to think this? The Yazata managed to hit gold on DriveThru with a book giving the basics of their pantheon, and they a) weren't in the core and b) aren't well-known in the West; it'd seem reasonable to suspect an in-depth Yazata book could do well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-08T13:58:07-08:00","id":26018,"title":"","content":"While I think the idea is nice - it was a shame that Ragnarok was the only book that got into that kind of detail for a Pantheon - I'm still a bit hopeful that the 2e approach will do a better job at presenting the Pantheons in the first place. Better initial representation means less need for this sort of book.<br /> <br /> This would mean you could do books that are less Pantheon specific (which ultimately reduces their appeal to the whole audience) and build books more around themes. A book about &quot;The End of the World.&quot; Lets you talk about the included mythologies that have strong end-times elements in some detail, but also discuss the generally mythological concept and how to use it in your game that would be useful regardless of which Pantheons you've got going. A book on &quot;State Religions&quot; lets you look at the Pantheons in the context of ones that were focused around the large ancient empires but have long since fallen out of practice, the ones that have only recently fallen from that position, the ones that are still dominant religions, and the ones that seem like they might be edging towards a come-back. This is again going to have a lot more inherent material for some of the Pantheons than others, but it can apply to almost any game that is going to deal with the repercussions of the Pantheons mixing with human affairs on a civilization level.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-08T14:27:34-08:00","id":26043,"title":"","content":"One of the things I loved about Scion: Ragnarok was that it showed that the Gods weren't locked in with their point of views. (with the help of scions anyway). I liked seeing the possibility that Fenris (and maybe others) could be redeemed and move on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-08T23:51:04-08:00","id":26338,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/25950-pantheon-sepcific-books?p=26018#post26018\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While I think the idea is nice - it was a shame that Ragnarok was the only book that got into that kind of detail for a Pantheon - I'm still a bit hopeful that the 2e approach will do a better job at presenting the Pantheons in the first place. Better initial representation means less need for this sort of book.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mm. I'm not sure of this. While a good initial presentation of a pantheon would make it easy to roleplay them, a well-done pantheon book would let us look &quot;under the hood&quot;, so to speak - explore the worlds they call their own, the various ways they engage with the modern World, the internal relationships and politics within the pantheon, etc. In short, getting a better, more in-depth understanding of them and their worlds.<br /> <br /> As to relevance... to be honest, at the level of a splat, I don't think that's so much an issue. There's a place for an indepth exploration, as with a splatbook, and a wide-ranging exploration, as with themed books. In the best of both worlds, we'd see both. (There likely _is_ a cut-off point for how relevant a sourcebook is to a buying audience, but I think a splatbook is a good distance from that point - eyeballing WW's various splatbooks on DriveThru, they all seem reasonably popular.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/51-marin","name":"marin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2019"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-09T00:16:43-08:00","id":26345,"title":"","content":"The splatbooks are usually fairly successful (but the number of them isn't directly correlated to how popular they were, lots of cWOD ones have pretty bad reputations), but they're also a lot shorter than something like Ragnarok. Things like the cWOD splat books, the Exalted 1e splat books, and the Requiem Clanbooks all clock in ~100 pages. Ragnarok is ~240. The Order books for Awakening and the Covenant books for Requiem are the only books that come close to that kind of detail on a single splat (well not counting the Exalted core books for non-solars); but the Orders and Covenants both were in serious need of expansion and played a much more important role in the day-to-day for the PCs.<br /> <br /> The problem with individual splat books for the Pantheons is that there's going to be a lot of them. If we see twenty of them, that's a lot of books. If we see thirty? That's also as many as VtM, and WtA individual Revised splatbooks combined (13 Clan, 3 Sect, 13 Tribe [Auspices were one book]). It just seems like more of a pain to the authors, as they have to write all those books, and the customers since you're going to end up wanting to support all the Pantheons in the main books. I'm not confident that there's the support on either end for enough splat books to double what any other game line has done in a single edition.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-09T04:57:48-08:00","id":26423,"title":"","content":"Marin, the forums won't let me quote you derectly, but if they can manage to do complete books (splat books) for each pantheon, grand. If they can't, clubing together is far better than nothing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-09T10:21:54-08:00","id":26577,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/25950-pantheon-sepcific-books?p=26345#post26345\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The splatbooks are usually fairly successful (but the number of them isn't directly correlated to how popular they were, lots of cWOD ones have pretty bad reputations), but they're also a lot shorter than something like Ragnarok. Things like the cWOD splat books, the Exalted 1e splat books, and the Requiem Clanbooks all clock in ~100 pages. Ragnarok is ~240. The Order books for Awakening and the Covenant books for Requiem are the only books that come close to that kind of detail on a single splat (well not counting the Exalted core books for non-solars); but the Orders and Covenants both were in serious need of expansion and played a much more important role in the day-to-day for the PCs.</div> </div> </div> </div>I had a love/hate relationship with the clanbooks when I still played WoD. They're both good and bad and I suppose it really depends on the discipline of the writer responsible for the book. Some were like a collection of seemingly random facts about the clan plus a few dangling threads that could be perceived as adventure hooks, a smattering of crunch, and woefully inadequate descriptions of the internal politics. Others were on point, everything presented clearly, giving you a strong idea of who's who and what's what, defining a dozen or so good adventure hooks, and providing good crunch that fits with the clan's modus operandi.<br /> <br /> Ragnarok, on the other hand, is sort of a crash course in how the pantheon works, who does what (on both sides of the fence), how it can fall apart and how it can fix itself (depending your particular interpretation of what &quot;fall apart&quot; and &quot;fix&quot; mean). But there is not, to be frank, a solid 240 pages of material for every pantheon. Some would feel a little empty at 100. Some pantheons just don't have all that many adventure hooks, all of the crazy interpersonal relationships between deities, all of the moving pieces in the machine of apocalypse, etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/25950-pantheon-sepcific-books?p=26345#post26345\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem with individual splat books for the Pantheons is that there's going to be a lot of them. If we see twenty of them, that's a lot of books. If we see thirty? That's also as many as VtM, and WtA individual Revised splatbooks combined (13 Clan, 3 Sect, 13 Tribe [Auspices were one book]). It just seems like more of a pain to the authors, as they have to write all those books, and the customers since you're going to end up wanting to support all the Pantheons in the main books. I'm not confident that there's the support on either end for enough splat books to double what any other game line has done in a single edition.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is that most groups would probably only be interested in pantheon books relevant to their table. Most tables tend to shuffle through the same half dozen pantheons. I don't know if the player/buyer base is large enough to support an effort for each pantheon. Some, particularly the ones that won't be in the core books, will be niche.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-09T11:03:00-08:00","id":26607,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/25950-pantheon-sepcific-books?p=26577#post26577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I had a love/hate relationship with the clanbooks when I still played WoD.</div> </div> </div> </div>They only people I know that feel differently just hate all of them. I'm with you on the love/hate thing with the cWOD splatbooks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But there is not, to be frank, a solid 240 pages of material for every pantheon. Some would feel a little empty at 100. Some pantheons just don't have all that many adventure hooks, all of the crazy interpersonal relationships between deities, all of the moving pieces in the machine of apocalypse, etc. </div> </div> </div>I'm biased in that I spend a lot of time with people involved in classics and religion, but any of the real world Pantheons that were used in Scion 1e could easily fill 240 pages. If anything, many of them needed it more because most people are familiar with the Ragnarok myth; so highlighting it as a story hook is a bit obvious. Bringing out all the interesting story hooks in the other Pantheons is a bit more important precisely because more people aren't as familiar with some of the major elements that you can use for that purpose.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The problem is that most groups would probably only be interested in pantheon books relevant to their table. Most tables tend to shuffle through the same half dozen pantheons. I don't know if the player/buyer base is large enough to support an effort for each pantheon. Some, particularly the ones that won't be in the core books, will be niche. </div> </div> </div>The problem with that assumption is that Scion 1e only had the half-dozen core Pantheons; so that's what people would tend to default to. The stated intention of the new team is to have a much larger set of available Pantheons; which changes the dynamics considerably. For example, in my experience with the cWOD you have 13 (or more/less depending on your choice of setting/time period) core Tribes. There are some players that gravitate to a small selection of them, but in most games I've seen the vast majority of them get used between the ST and the players. Even if a group tends to only use a few Tribes at once, they tend to switch it up to a different cluster of them next time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-09T11:42:07-08:00","id":26629,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/25950-pantheon-sepcific-books?p=26607#post26607\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm biased in that I spend a lot of time with people involved in classics and religion, but any of the real world Pantheons that were used in Scion 1e could easily fill 240 pages. If anything, many of them needed it more because most people are familiar with the Ragnarok myth; so highlighting it as a story hook is a bit obvious. Bringing out all the interesting story hooks in the other Pantheons is a bit more important precisely because more people aren't as familiar with some of the major elements that you can use for that purpose.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think you couldn't write 240 pages on each pantheon. I just don't think you could do so and keep it within the scope of relevance for the purposes of the game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/25950-pantheon-sepcific-books?p=26607#post26607\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem with that assumption is that Scion 1e only had the half-dozen core Pantheons; so that's what people would tend to default to. The stated intention of the new team is to have a much larger set of available Pantheons; which changes the dynamics considerably. For example, in my experience with the cWOD you have 13 (or more/less depending on your choice of setting/time period) core Tribes. There are some players that gravitate to a small selection of them, but in most games I've seen the vast majority of them get used between the ST and the players. Even if a group tends to only use a few Tribes at once, they tend to switch it up to a different cluster of them next time.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think that even if they double the number of pantheons in the core books you'll see many tables shifting from their typical modus. Which pantheons they are, of course, could change.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-09T12:57:14-08:00","id":26682,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dayen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/25950-pantheon-sepcific-books?p=26629#post26629\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think you couldn't write 240 pages on each pantheon. I just don't think you could do so and keep it within the scope of relevance for the purposes of the game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Based on what exactly? Which of the Pantheons from 1e are from such a sparse mythological basis that you can't get 240 pages out of them? The Order and Covenant books for the nWOD are that long and they're about completely fictional organizations. WOD: Chicago is ~420 pages long... and about a single city and the fictional residents within.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't think that even if they double the number of pantheons in the core books you'll see many tables shifting from their typical modus. Which pantheons they are, of course, could change. </div> </div> </div>Again, what is this based on? In my experience when you start getting core splats in the 10+ range, people don't tend to stick to 4-6 of them as a group. Again, individual players might. An ST might be really into one of them. But I haven't seen the effect you're talking about. When I've played WtA, I've seen all the Tribes get played over time. When I've played MtA, I've seen all the Traditions get played over time. Etc. Are there splats that are less popular in my group? Absolutely. But that has always been the direction I've seen things go rather than groups latch onto a handful of the options and just stick to those all the time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-09T13:54:13-08:00","id":26717,"title":"","content":"O<span style=\"color:#333333\"><span style=\"font-family:lucida grande\">ne thing that Onyx Path could do, is release these as PDFs only at first to see how many are interested, and if they do well. they can see about making them available in print. I could be wrong, and maybe someone can correct me, but I do believe pdfs are cheaper to make then other books.<br /> <br /> I will agree though that -all- the pantheons could easily get a book to themselves. Of course, I could be biased being that I am a polytheist and read blogs from other polytheists. So I can see all of them getting their own book to explain things for people that are unfamilar to that pantheon or just are interested in scion's take. I for example would like be willing to buy a Tuatha book about the gods I worship; though to be fair I am interested in books like this for all pantheons not just mine.</span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1381-herkles","name":"herkles","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"325"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-09T14:16:32-08:00","id":26728,"title":"","content":"Hmm..a 240 page book on a single pantheon? Yeah. Doable since it's like focusing on a universe-wide idea. However, I don't think it'd be profitable though. Have to remember the business-side of things and all that it takes to publish a single book.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Mankind was once an endangered species. It will likely be so again. And mankind will only have itself to blame.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/144-shadowflame","name":"Shadowflame","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar144_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-09T15:32:11-08:00","id":26765,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>herkles</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/25950-pantheon-sepcific-books?p=26717#post26717\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I could be wrong, and maybe someone can correct me, but I do believe pdfs are cheaper to make then other books.</div> </div> </div> </div>With the current scheme, print books don't add a huge cost for them. Onyx Path, outside of books that get Kickstarter drives for deluxe copies, uses a Print-on-Demand service through their PDF distributor. The only extra work (though it is work) is to modify the document for both PDF and POD distribution. From there DriveThruRPG essentially takes care of the rest.<br /> <br /> There is some added cost of Onyx Path to put something out as a PDF and a POD book, but it isn't nearly as expensive as it used to be to have both now that POD service is where it is.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-10T04:40:46-08:00","id":27116,"title":"","content":"Between the Myths assosiated with each pantheon and the culture those myths came from, yes there are 240 good pages to be had for each Pantheon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-10T04:44:05-08:00","id":27119,"title":"","content":"The system lets me only write a sentence or two at a time, then kicks me out, so forgive the multipost. But let me add that each pantheon comes with unique issues and associates. Properly covering these can easily fill 500 pages!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-10T14:05:25-08:00","id":27518,"title":"","content":"240 good pages? Probably. 240 financially viable pages? I doubt it. Especially if it means taking authors away from other, more generally desirable, projects.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Need some online tools, including a Scion/Exalted battle wheel? <a href=\"http://rpg-tools.org/generators/index.php\" target=\"_blank\">http://rpg-tools.org/generators/index.php</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/829-james-mcmurray","name":"James McMurray","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-10T23:42:04-08:00","id":27805,"title":"","content":"Why not 2-Pantheon books? Match less popular (\u00abfinancially viable\u00bb) pantheons with the star players. They could each have their own part in the book without touching each other necessarily. If you take out the Ragnar\u00f6k campaign lengthy descriptions to focus on hooks for each tier it might be doable. It would make 5 add-on books instead of 10.<br /> <br /> Of course, if you do 150-200 pages softcovers (or PDF) of unique pantheons it could work instead of 300 pages of 2 pantheons. Keep in mind that with Origins/Hero the pantheons might be more covered than they were in 1ed. So it's all extra fluff.<br /> <br /> Could also be some Kickstarters to get the bare minimum to make these financially viable. I agree that Scion: Ragnar\u00f6k is a life saver as an ST even if I am comfortable with the Aesir myths. If I had picked my band any other pantheon it would have been hel. I didn't take everything from the book (my campaign has been running for 5 years) but I feel the other pantheons also deserve a little specific attention.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1625-papy","name":"Papy","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T04:51:19-08:00","id":27896,"title":"","content":"Pairing up the Pantheons to let the popular pantheons sell the book while the less popular pantheon gets coverage seems wise to me. I'm sure you can sell books on the Norse, Greek, Egyptian, Celtic, and Japanese, pantheons. Place the Chinese pantheon with the Japanese and that book sells.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T09:07:49-08:00","id":28023,"title":"","content":"I'm curious... how do any of us, including the Onyx Path folks... know what is and is not a popular Pantheon? It isn't like we can point to anything that shows a specific difference in sales between different Pantheon specific material of a roughly equal nature (comparing Ragnarok to the Yazata just isn't viable). Which Pantheons are popular at our gaming tables is just anecdote; not sales data.<br /> <br /> I'd also imagine that the present RPG marketplace is less tolerant of this sort of scheme. In the old days when your options were book or nothing, combining topics like that was fairly viable. Now with a PDF first approach to distribution - where you can easily separate out distinct sections of a book and allow the downloading of each separately - people are going to see right through this and have the ability to express the perfectly valid complaint of, &quot;you're making me buy something I don't want to get what I want when you don't have to do that.&quot;<br /> <br /> The PR damage seems wildly unnecessary.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-12-11T11:40:04-08:00","id":28149,"title":"","content":"I'll allow that you could write 240 pages about any of the pantheons, but I really would not want to see someone try for all of them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/25950-pantheon-sepcific-books?p=28023#post28023\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm curious... how do any of us, including the Onyx Path folks... know what is and is not a popular Pantheon? It isn't like we can point to anything that shows a specific difference in sales between different Pantheon specific material of a roughly equal nature (comparing Ragnarok to the Yazata just isn't viable). Which Pantheons are popular at our gaming tables is just anecdote; not sales data.<br /> <br /> I'd also imagine that the present RPG marketplace is less tolerant of this sort of scheme. In the old days when your options were book or nothing, combining topics like that was fairly viable. Now with a PDF first approach to distribution - where you can easily separate out distinct sections of a book and allow the downloading of each separately - people are going to see right through this and have the ability to express the perfectly valid complaint of, &quot;you're making me buy something I don't want to get what I want when you don't have to do that.&quot;<br /> <br /> The PR damage seems wildly unnecessary.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is why pantheon-specific books are likely to be a bad idea and why we probably ought to shelve it. Because many people actually practice these faiths, they're going to feel slighted by any attempt to actually determine how financially viable their particular religion is, through the lens of a tabletop role-playing game. Actually categorizing them as &quot;second tier&quot; in a pseudo-official way would just be rubbing salt in the wound.<br /> <br /> Ragnarok was a great book, interesting and valuable to most tables. I don't think that's the sort of lightning you can bottle twice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/889-dayen","name":"Dayen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar889_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"330"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-15T14:48:14-08:00","id":72832,"title":"","content":"Will all due respect, Dayen, I'm having a hard time not reading some of your earlier statements -- &quot;I'll allow that you could write 240 pages about any of the pantheons, but I really would not want to see someone try for all of them.&quot; and such -- to be more likely to &quot;attempt to actually determine how financially viable their particular religion is&quot;, as by only focusing on fleshing out certain pantheons you are deciding would not &quot;feel a little empty at 100&quot; and would be financially viable is doing exactly what you're trying to say focusing on all the pantheons used in the games would be.<br /> <br /> Also, sorry to call you out specifically -- you're just the one making the majority of posts to the effect of &quot;But you couldn't make 240 pg+ books for all the pantheons&quot;.<br /> <br /> Providing sourcebooks for all the pantheons ensures that there's space for details like the ones being talked about in the thread re: Kali and Shiva and the fact that they were portrayed entirely for the destructive aspects of their personality; it gives space to discuss things like real life figures being adopted as gods by Chinese mythology and religion -- in a game designed by and for Western developers and audiences, audiences where a lot of people are already going to have a hard time with polytheist viewpoints , it's a good place to look at how pantheons interact with their cultures and with our own culture.<br /> <br /> For instance, Norse mythology has gods you pray to for specific things, even if you don't like these gods; Greek mythology is basically full of gods and goddesses who never grew up, mentally or emotionally, but still have the power to wreck your lives if they want. Basically, Western-based mythology is full of gods as Alpha Jerks, who you kind of have to kowtow to in order to survive.<br /> <br /> In contrast, Eastern mythology tends to focus on harmony and duty -- their gods tend to be more mature and levelheaded in how they respond to challenges. When Brahma and Vishnu were arguing about who was stronger, they didn't ultimately decide one was; Shiva was born, and the three of them came to the realization that they were all powerful in their own ways and they were all equally great. Likewise, there isn't a tendency to try and place the world into a concrete &quot;This is good&quot; and &quot;This is bad&quot;; everything exists in harmony, and even chaotic figures, like the Monkey King have their place and purpose. Exploring these things can make for a richer game, and has the added bonus of showing respect to the real world religion, where it chooses to provide accurate in depth information, instead of simplifying &quot;this god rules over destruction and is therefore evil&quot; or &quot;this god is a little different from the rest and so tends to get shunned&quot; the way 1e has.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2230-murder-chan","name":"Murder-chan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2230_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"73"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-02-15T15:37:51-08:00","id":72849,"title":"","content":"The problem with that argument is that the current design philosophy as explained by the development team is meant to address a lot of that in the four core Scion books. They have stated as a goal that doing a better job of having each Pantheon be more true to their cultural origins and less forced into a single mold. The new core set of books are supposed to have far more page spaced dedicated to things like the cultures of each Pantheon and addressing variations (both intra-Pantheon variations like how Zeus and Jupiter are the same guy, and inter-Pantheon differences like Pantheons that are more in-line with Scions as literal children of gods and ones that would operate more on special people that ascend to divine status).<br /> <br /> So, ideally, we don't need Pantheon specific books because the new design philosophy is supposed to address those concerns in the main books as is; even with the expansion of the core Pantheon set to 10.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}